import{_ as r,o as t,c as a,a0 as s}from"./chunks/framework.D4fUSYzX.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The homepage hero is a real herdr capture","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0028-the-homepage-hero-is-a-real-herdr-capture.md","filePath":"adr/0028-the-homepage-hero-is-a-real-herdr-capture.md"}'),o={name:"adr/0028-the-homepage-hero-is-a-real-herdr-capture.md"};function h(i,e,n,d,c,p){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="the-homepage-hero-is-a-real-herdr-capture" tabindex="-1">The homepage hero is a real herdr capture <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-homepage-hero-is-a-real-herdr-capture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The homepage hero is a real herdr capture&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted.</p><p>The documentation homepage shows one hero image of the control plane at work. The six operation screenshots already render the control plane in a plain terminal from a deterministic PTY fixture, and a drift test guards them. The homepage should show the control plane in its real container, a herdr workspace, so a first visitor sees the product the way an operator runs it.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The hero is generated by a script, <code>npm run hero</code>. The script drives a real, isolated herdr: a fresh server in a temporary directory with its own socket, home, and config. It never touches the operator&#39;s live herdr session. The script makes two workspaces, the way a real run stands: the control plane runs full width in its own workspace over the same deterministic fixture the operation screenshots use, and the running ticket&#39;s handoff environment is its own workspace on its own checkout, unfocused. The shot shows only the plane; the agent is a fact of herdr&#39;s sidebar, not a pane beside the plane. The script selects the running ticket in the control plane, so the Detail pane shows the ticket the agent works, and it reports the agent&#39;s pane to herdr as a working agent, so the sidebar carries the agent&#39;s workspace and the running state the ticket&#39;s row reads. An attached herdr client renders the plane&#39;s workspace, and the script captures the client&#39;s screen with the shared ANSI renderer into <code>docs/public/hero.png</code>.</p><p>The hero is refreshed by hand. It is not in the drift test.</p><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered Options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Draw a mock herdr chrome around the fixture. This was rejected: a mock drifts from the real herdr, and it costs a second renderer to keep.</li><li>Put the hero in the drift test. This was rejected: herdr owns the chrome (the sidebar, the pane borders, the tab bar), and the chrome changes when herdr changes. A drift test would fail on every herdr upgrade for a reason outside this project.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The control plane pane in the hero is deterministic: it comes from the same fixture as the operation screenshots, and its <code>gh</code> and <code>herdr</code> lookups resolve to the fixture&#39;s stubs. The agent&#39;s workspace is a stub: its pane holds a static process, and its state comes from the script&#39;s report. Only the herdr chrome varies, and it varies with herdr.</li><li>The hero is a manual artifact. Refresh it when herdr changes or when the main view changes in a way the homepage should reflect.</li><li>The capture is isolated. A failed or interrupted run leaves no herdr state outside the temporary directory, and the operator&#39;s session is never read or written.</li><li>The image lives in the site&#39;s static asset directory, <code>docs/public/</code>, and the homepage references it by site path.</li></ul>',10)])])}const m=r(o,[["render",h]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
